11
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:30,671
Now first of all, what we can immediately

12
00:00:30,671 --> 00:00:33,161
see when we open this in our IDE,

13
00:00:33,883 --> 00:00:35,569
in Visual Studio Code in my case,

14
00:00:36,196 --> 00:00:39,086
is that the IDE itself is showing an error

15
00:00:39,086 --> 00:00:39,231
here.

16
00:00:39,889 --> 00:00:42,844
It's showing red squiggly lines on content,

17
00:00:43,503 --> 00:00:45,221
and whenever you got something like this,

18
00:00:45,510 --> 00:00:47,855
that's of course a very clear indicator that

19
00:00:47,855 --> 00:00:49,011
something might be wrong.

20
00:00:49,927 --> 00:00:52,994
Occasionally the IDE might be wrong, it

21
00:00:52,994 --> 00:00:55,451
might not be able to understand your code

22
00:00:55,483 --> 00:00:58,404
fully, but in the majority of cases you are

23
00:00:58,452 --> 00:00:58,661
wrong.

24
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:02,265
The best thing to do if you get these red

25
00:01:02,394 --> 00:01:05,213
squiggly lines is to simply hover over them

26
00:01:05,325 --> 00:01:06,511
and see what the problem is.

27
00:01:07,104 --> 00:01:09,731
And here we see that an argument of type

28
00:01:09,731 --> 00:01:12,407
string is not assignable to a parameter of

29
00:01:12,455 --> 00:01:13,672
type uint8 array.

30
00:01:14,297 --> 00:01:15,627
So we have a type error here,

31
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:19,296
and we would also get this error message if

32
00:01:19,344 --> 00:01:21,555
we would try to run this code, by the way.

33
00:01:22,644 --> 00:01:24,070
But we don't even need to do that here.

34
00:01:24,647 --> 00:01:26,777
We clearly are passing the wrong kind of

35
00:01:26,809 --> 00:01:30,497
data to write file, because if we inspect

36
00:01:30,497 --> 00:01:33,064
writeFile, we see that the data it wants

37
00:01:33,288 --> 00:01:35,470
should be of type uint8 array,

38
00:01:36,577 --> 00:01:39,625
and the content I have here is a string, and

39
00:01:39,641 --> 00:01:41,806
that's clearly not a uint8 array.

40
00:01:42,833 --> 00:01:44,886
In the example code I provided you,

41
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,421
I'm essentially fetching some user input

42
00:01:47,421 --> 00:01:47,533
here.

43
00:01:48,223 --> 00:01:51,239
Then I have this parseBuffer helper function

44
00:01:51,511 --> 00:01:54,607
which just uses the text decoder to decode

45
00:01:54,784 --> 00:01:55,217
the buffer.

46
00:01:55,906 --> 00:01:58,215
And what I'm doing here is that I'm using

47
00:01:58,327 --> 00:02:01,100
the decoded content as an argument for

48
00:02:01,212 --> 00:02:01,725
write_file.

49
00:02:02,478 --> 00:02:05,203
Now write_file doesn't want that decoded

50
00:02:05,203 --> 00:02:07,528
content though. It wants the raw byte

51
00:02:07,528 --> 00:02:07,912
content.

52
00:02:08,521 --> 00:02:11,759
So the fix here is simple. We pass input

53
00:02:11,791 --> 00:02:14,452
buffer like this as an argument. And now

54
00:02:14,741 --> 00:02:16,119
this error is gone.

55
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:19,341
Now this program will still have a couple of

56
00:02:19,486 --> 00:02:21,698
other problems, but at least we got rid of

57
00:02:21,794 --> 00:02:24,925
this error. And the simple core takeaway

58
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:26,677
from this lecture therefore is

59
00:02:27,256 --> 00:02:29,731
that if your IDE is showing an error to you,

60
00:02:30,165 --> 00:02:30,937
you should simply

61
00:02:31,515 --> 00:02:33,991
analyze it, read the error description,

62
00:02:34,569 --> 00:02:37,784
and in the vast majority of cases, the error

63
00:02:37,784 --> 00:02:39,680
description will be pretty clear, will make

64
00:02:39,729 --> 00:02:42,091
sense, and will allow you to fix the error.

65
00:02:42,445 --> 00:02:44,277
So let's now move on to the next problem.
